Changes made for ANPASIDE
-------------------------

Removed:

  - the loader's own user interface: app list, jad/jar downloads, installer
    dialogs, per app settings, profile and key mapping screens, file picker,
    ACRA crash reporting and the Room database of installed midlets
  - 3D: JSR-184 (M3G), Mascot Capsule and the vendor wrappers around it,
    together with all of the project's native code
  - camera capture, bluetooth, and the audio format converter built on
    mobile-ffmpeg
  - the VIBRATE, RECORD_AUDIO and POST_NOTIFICATIONS permissions and the
    microphone hardware feature from the manifest: MIDletPascal has no
    vibration API, its media support is playback only, and the only Nokia UI
    class the compiler emits is FullCanvas, so nothing the compiler emits
    reaches them; INTERNET is the only permission kept. The Nokia
    SoftNotification classes stay in the tree but are unreachable from compiled
    midlets, so on Android 13+ their permission request fails and post() throws
    SoftNotificationException

Added or changed:

  - MidletRunner, the whole public surface of the module: it dexes a jar with
    the bundled dx, writes the IDE-selected display and keypad profile, passes
    those launch settings directly to the emulator process and starts its
    activity, so nothing appears between the Run button and the running midlet
  - the emulator keeps converted midlets, profiles and record stores in
    <external files dir>/emulator instead of a shared J2ME-Loader directory
  - the system Back action exits the embedded midlet directly instead of
    opening the loader options menu
  - camera capture returns a silent player instead of throwing, and
    platformRequest no longer hands over to another installed midlet
  - JSR-75 file connections accept the "file://c:/dir/name" form, which has two
    slashes where the specification wants three and therefore parses the drive
    letter as the URL authority. Upstream reads the path alone and loses the
    root, so such a path resolves one directory too high, or fails outright when
    it names a file directly in the drive root. The MIDletPascal library
    Lib_jsr75i builds every one of its URLs that way
  - the JSR-75 e:/ drive always maps into <external files dir>/emulator/fs/e.
    Upstream maps it to the shared storage root while the app has the legacy
    storage view, which the IDE cannot write to without a storage permission it
    does not request
  - Canvas takes the offscreen buffer lock while it obtains the Graphics it
    paints a frame with, fixing an upstream race in which a resize arriving
    from the ui thread during the first frame left that Graphics bound to a
    discarded bitmap and the canvas black for the rest of the run
  - Canvas releases a pointer that was pressed on the canvas when it is lifted
    off the canvas, and every pressed pointer when the system cancels the
    gesture. Upstream drops both releases, so a midlet keeps that finger down
    for good, which an on-screen button held with a second finger runs into
    as soon as the finger slides off the canvas
  - MicroActivity hides the system bars before the first layout and starts the
    midlet only once the window is laid out in the requested orientation, and
    the canvas takes its first size from that layout. Upstream starts the
    midlet in onCreate, where Display reports the screen without the
    navigation bar; a midlet that sizes its buffers once, as every
    MIDletPascal program does, then drew past the bottom of them as soon as
    the bars were hidden and the canvas grew
  - MicroActivity lays the window out into the display cutout by default.
    Upstream leaves that off unless its "Add cutout area" setting is on, a
    setting this module has no screen for, so with the bars hidden every
    midlet got a black band the height of the camera cutout
  - consumer-rules.pro keeps every class a midlet can resolve by name, and the
    gson-mapped emulator profile, in any application that shrinks this module
    with R8; upstream ships its rules with the application instead
